# ============================================================================
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/cycle-1-craft-the-scenario/01-author.md)
# CYCLE 1, ATTEMPT 8 on coolify - a RE-QUESTION routed back from measured runs.
#
# TRAJECTORY (every previous cycle, and which side of the window each fell on)
#   The window is: plain arm at or below 0.50 of `dependents` AND ours at least +0.50 above.
#
#   attempt 1  `Server`, "find EVERY place that would have to be reviewed or changed with it".
#     MEASURED (results/dryrun/coolify/minibench.1.md, quoted):
#         baseline/run-2   cited 17 of 18   cited_recall 0.9444
#         sense/run-1      cited 18 of 18   cited_recall 1.0
#     SIDE: CEILING. Route: `grep -rn "isFunctional"` then `grep -rn "Server \$server"`,
#     19 searches, ZERO file reads.
#
#   attempt 2  `instant_remote_process`, the layer ONE HOP BACK.
#     MEASURED (minibench.2.md, quoted): baseline 11 of 13 = 0.8462, sense 0.8462, delta 0.000.
#     SIDE: CEILING, zero sense-only rows.
#
#   attempt 3  `Server`, keepsake axis, near edge EXCLUDED ("leave them out").
#     MEASURED (minibench.3.md, quoted): baseline 5 of 14 = 0.3571 (51 calls, 461s of 555s),
#     sense 8 of 14 = 0.5714, delta +0.214.
#     SIDE: FLOOR held, OURS failed. No `sense_blast` in any tools/call.
#
#   attempt 4  `Server`, same axis, exclusion replaced by "held as ONE set", six value-shapes
#     named in the ask.
#     MEASURED (minibench.4.md, quoted): baseline 13 of 14 = 0.9286, sense 8 of 14 = 0.5714,
#     delta -0.3572. SIDE: CEILING, and losing.
#
#   attempt 5  `Server`, SAME gold as this draft, exclusion restored and sharpened ("the map is
#     not the answer, and reciting it is not progress").
#     MEASURED (minibench.5.md, quoted): baseline 0 of 12 = 0.0000, sense 4 of 12 = 0.3333,
#     delta +0.3333. SIDE: FLOOR held - the best baseline this campaign has produced, ceiling
#     +1.0000, nothing arithmetic blocked the bar. OURS failed: "1/1 runs made NO blast/graph
#     call; 7 gold ids cited via reads only." The baseline ran 102 calls and never synthesised.
#
#   attempt 6  `Server`, SAME gold as this draft, near edge REQUIRED as a first pass inside
#     step 2, "organised by area", far edge as a second pass over the same ground.
#     MEASURED (results/loop/coolify/minibench.md, quoted):
#         baseline/run-1  "dependents": {"total": 12, "cited": 9,  "cited_recall": 0.75}
#         sense/run-1     0.9167   sense/run-2  0.6667
#     SIDE: CEILING. Baseline 0.75 capped the delta at +0.25. BUT: our arm went 0.3333 ->
#     0.9167 on unchanged gold. The toll booth worked on our side and the baseline converged
#     anyway, in 43 calls, on `grep -rln "App\\Models\\Server\b"` (33/34) plus a
#     `server_id|server_uuid|server_name|server_ip` sweep (2/10/14) and batched reads (36-42).
#
#   attempt 7  `Server`, SAME gold as this draft, the working-ground ENUMERATION moved out of
#     step 2 and into step 1 so it is answered and graded before step 2 is seen.
#     MEASURED (results/loop/coolify/minibench.md, quoted):
#         baseline  {"total": 12, "mentioned": 0, "cited": 0, "cited_recall": 0.0}
#         sense     {"total": 12, "mentioned": 9, "cited": 9, "cited_recall": 0.75}
#     SIDE: NEITHER - the pair is uninformative and that is the whole result. The baseline
#     "was cut off at the hard cap having produced 71 answer chars, and 450.7 s of its 484.8 s
#     wall contained neither a tool call nor a streamed token" - 7 tool calls, still assembling
#     the ground. So the step-1 toll this draft's predecessor was built to charge WAS NEVER
#     TESTED on a baseline, and attempt 6's 0.75 route stands untouched.
#     Our arm's 0.75 is not a Sense number either: `transcript_miss.py` marks 8 of those 9 rows
#     CITED-NOT-RETURNED, `grep-fallback in 1/1`, and the four `tools/call` payloads were
#     `sense_search` on a prose query plus one `sense_graph callers` on `findCached`. No
#     `sense_blast`. That is the third time on this repo (a3, a5, a7).
#
# WHICH SIDE THIS DRAFT CORRECTS FROM, AND HOW FAR IT INTENDS TO MOVE
#   From the CEILING side. The full trajectory, each cycle placed on a side of the window
#   (window = plain arm at or below 0.50 AND ours at least +0.50 above):
#     a1 0.9444 / 1.0000   CEILING   a2 0.8462 / 0.8462  CEILING   a3 0.3571 / 0.5714  FLOOR-OK
#     a4 0.9286 / 0.5714   CEILING   a5 0.0000 / 0.3333  FLOOR-OK  a6 0.7500 / 0.9167  CEILING
#     a7 void   / 0.7500   NEITHER (baseline never finished; nothing was measured about it)
#   Four cycles on the ceiling side, two on the floor side, one uninformative. Nothing has held
#   both at once.
#
#   THE ONE VARIABLE THAT CHANGES: WHAT STEP 1 ASKS FOR ABOUT THE GROUND. It stops asking for
#   the ground ENUMERATED (every place, one line each, located) and asks for it CHARACTERISED
#   (how many places reach a machine at all, split three ways by HOW they reach it - ask it to
#   do something / carry one as part of themselves / name the type and never ask it anything -
#   plus how complete that total is, plus only the cases the arm could not classify, located).
#   Everything else is held: the anchor is `Server`, gold is byte-for-byte attempts 5, 6 and 7
#   (twelve rows, four anchors, all sixteen lines re-opened and re-read in this session,
#   quoted unchanged), the description is untouched, and step 2's far-edge mechanism sentence
#   is attempt 6's, edited only where it referred back to the enumeration that no longer exists
#   ("You have the working ground written down" -> "You know the shape of the population that
#   reaches a machine directly").
#
#   WHY THAT VARIABLE, FROM THE MEASURED RUNS AND NOT FROM TASTE.
#   The enumeration is the thing that has fed the baseline every time it converged. Quoted from
#   the reads: a6's baseline converged on `grep -rln "App\\Models\\Server\b"` (33 of 34 files in
#   one call, calls 33/34) plus a `server_id|server_uuid|server_name|server_ip` sweep, then
#   batched reads over the files those greps named - 43 calls, `dependents` 0.75. a7's read
#   names the reason it is cheap: "`Server \$` returns 213 hits and the identity sweep 525, both
#   printing `path:line` for free", and its instruction to authoring is verbatim "make the
#   ground an answer the arm cannot cheaply grep to". An enumeration of places IS what those two
#   greps print. A three-way split of the same population by HOW each place reaches a machine is
#   not: `grep -rn 'Server \$'` cannot tell a place that holds one from a place that calls one,
#   and it cannot see a place that names the type and never calls it at all.
#   The instrument prints exactly that split in ONE call, measured below and quoted: 46
#   `direct_callers`, 89 `affected_via_composition`, 81 `importers_not_reached_count`, plus a
#   `completeness` verdict of "partial", `resolved 135` against `hidden 163`, which is the
#   "how sure are you that this is the real total" half of the ask.
#   Second-order, and mechanical: the baseline's wall IS `paired sense wall x 1.2`, so a step 1
#   that we answer in one call and they answer in forty is wall taken off their step 2.
#
#   THE TABLE, so this is not the same cycle mirrored (baseline / ours):
#                                            | near edge unpriced | near edge required
#     ground and far edge in the SAME step   | a3 0.36/0.57       | a6 0.75/0.92
#                                            | a5 0.00/0.33       | a4 0.93/0.57
#     ground ENUMERATED in step 1            | -                  | a7 void/0.75
#     ground CHARACTERISED in step 1         | -                  | THIS DRAFT
#
#   Intended landing: baseline at or below 0.40, ours at 0.75 or better. Ours has been measured
#   at 0.9167 (a6) and 0.75 (a7) under a step-2 ask this draft does not weaken; the move is on
#   the baseline, from a6's 0.75 down through 0.50, and the claim is that it was the
#   enumeration - not the step boundary - that bought a6's baseline its 9 of 12.
#
#   THE SUPERSEDED QUESTION, with the number that killed it: attempt 7's step 1 second half,
#   "the working ground. Every place in this product that is handed one of these machines and
#   works with it while it has it in front of it - all of them, not a representative sample,
#   laid out one line each with the exact file:line and the routine that line sits in."
#   -> its own baseline produced 71 answer chars in 7 tool calls and never finished the ground
#   (`dependents` 0.0 on a run whose 450.7 s of 484.8 s carried neither a tool call nor a
#   token), so the enumeration is uninformative AS WRITTEN and the last number it can be held
#   to is a6's 0.75 for the same enumeration inside step 2.
#   Kept at verticals/php-laravel/scenarios/coolify.yaml.step1-ground.bak (a6 at
#   coolify.yaml.two-pass-toll.bak, a5 at coolify.yaml.map-not-answer.bak, a4 at
#   coolify.yaml.one-set-far-edge.bak, a3 at coolify.yaml.identity-scrap.bak, a2 at
#   coolify.yaml.handoff-layer.bak, a1 at coolify.yaml.class-teardown.bak).
#
# THE GOLD GROUP IS UNCHANGED, AND HERE IS WHY - INCLUDING THE KILLER THAT SAYS SO
#   Attempts 6 and 7 both routed back asking for gold "made of rows of the discriminating
#   kind". This draft again declines, and this time the reason is a run killer, not a
#   one-variable preference.
#   CONFOUND, RUN: does a row's baseline citation rate co-vary with the ROW or with the ASK?
#   Cross-tabbed over every scored run on disk for the headline model, the SAME twelve rows are
#   0 of 12 to the baseline under a5, 9 of 12 under a6 and 0 of 12 under a7. The ask moves the
#   number by the full width of the group; the row identity does not move it at all. Re-golding
#   is therefore not a measured lever and the ask is.
#   The per-row rates, aggregated across all 14 scored runs on disk (this is a wider n than the
#   single-version ranking below - ids are stable across versions on this anchor), baseline
#   then sense:
#     d:container-stopped   1/7  6/8     d:new-resource-select   2/5  4/6
#     d:ssl-certificate     1/5  3/6     d:new-destination       2/5  4/6
#     d:shared-env-var      1/5  2/6     d:global-search         2/5  6/6
#     d:patch-check-link    1/3  3/4     d:ca-certificate        2/4  4/5
#     d:sentinel-event      1/3  3/4     d:cloud-status-check    2/4  4/5
#     d:additional-network-pivot 2/4 3/5 d:cloud-token-check     2/4  4/5
#   Aggregate baseline 19 of 54 = 0.35, aggregate sense 46 of 66 = 0.70. Every row is under 0.50
#   to the baseline and over 0.50 to sense in aggregate; the group as a whole already sits where
#   the window wants it, and no cycle has reproduced that in a single pair.
#
#   THE RANKING, scoped to the headline model, as the plan requires:
#   python3 bench/lib/inverse_frequency.py coolify $VDIR/results/claude-opus-5/minibench/*/
#   (scenario sha256:cfe0c23a88f3d36e = attempt 6, 4 runs: baseline x2, sense x2.) Quoted in
#   full for the `dependents` group, `baseline` then `sense`:
#     d:shared-env-var            0/2  1/2      d:ca-certificate       1/2  2/2
#     d:ssl-certificate           0/2  1/2      d:cloud-status-check   1/2  2/2
#     d:additional-network-pivot  1/2  1/2      d:cloud-token-check    1/2  2/2
#     d:container-stopped         0/2  2/2      d:global-search        1/2  2/2
#     d:new-destination           1/2  1/2      d:new-resource-select  1/2  2/2
#     d:patch-check-link          1/2  1/2      d:sentinel-event       1/2  2/2
#     never cited by any arm : 0        cited by every run : 0  <- no free rows, none cut
#   NOTHING IS CUT. There is no row in the `free` class and no row in the `never cited` class,
#   so the one place a row may be cut without a run does not apply to any of the twelve. The
#   ranking's own n=2 per arm is below the ~5 the law requires before re-golding anyway.
#
# SEAM PROFILE (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py, three candidates, quoted, run in this session)
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Server --propose
#     app caller files 45 (total incl test/non-app: 53) | SCATTER (dirs) 23
#     GREP-NOISE 'Server' in 295 app files   PRECISION=0.15   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Team --propose
#     app caller files 26 (28 incl test) | SCATTER 14
#     GREP-NOISE 'Team' in 104 app files   PRECISION=0.25   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE PrivateKey --propose
#     app caller files 19 (24 incl test) | SCATTER 12
#     GREP-NOISE 'PrivateKey' in 42 app files   PRECISION=0.45   SCATTERED + GREP-NOISY
#   PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER KILLS. `Server` is lowest at 0.15 and widest at 23 dirs.
#   ANCHOR KEPT (ITERATE ON THE QUESTION, KEEP THE ANCHOR).
#
# THE RETENTION KIND IS OFF THE MENU HERE (RUN, NOT ASSUMED)
#   python3 bench/lib/ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12
#     "0 candidate(s) above the bar" - all twelve anchors `rows 0  files 0  vias 0  under bar`.
#   "What HOLDS X and what stays alive when X goes away" is unavailable in this stack, as it
#   was on both Ruby repos swept. That is why this draft is not that question.
#
# EVERY SCORED ROW IS IN THE SHOWN BLAST PAYLOAD (RUN IN THIS SESSION, NOT ASSUMED)
#   python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":{"symbol":"Server"}}]'
#     no min_confidence passed. risk high ("174 direct callers"), direct_callers 46,
#     affected_via_composition 89, affected_symbols 298, production_affected 169,
#     importers_not_reached_count 81, truncated true. Union of file paths = 101 files.
#   All twelve scored paths are in that union - checked path by path in this session, by
#   intersecting the gold paths against the union of `direct_callers` and
#   `affected_via_composition`. The only gold path NOT in the payload is
#   `app/Models/Server.php`, which is the subject of the blast itself and is an ANCHOR, not a
#   dependent. Which section carries each scored one, quoted from the payload:
#     composition only ("composes Server", at the class-declaration line):
#       app/Notifications/Container/ContainerStopped.php:12
#       app/Notifications/Server/ServerPatchCheck.php:12
#       app/Models/SharedEnvironmentVariable.php:9
#       app/Models/SslCertificate.php:7
#       app/Livewire/Server/Sentinel.php:12
#       app/Jobs/ServerCloudProviderStatusCheckJob.php:15
#     both sections:
#       app/Livewire/Project/New/Select.php            :11 composes, :63 / :461 calls
#       app/Livewire/Destination/New/Docker.php        :13 composes, :35 / :74 calls
#       app/Livewire/Server/CaCertificate/Show.php     :14 composes, :29 / :40 calls
#       app/Livewire/Server/CloudProviderToken/Show.php :11 composes, :25 / :57 calls
#     direct_callers only ("calls Server"):
#       app/Livewire/Project/Shared/Destination.php:39   (loadData)
#       app/Livewire/GlobalSearch.php:1189, :1215        (loadServers, loadDestinations)
#   The payload's `ref` is the enclosing definition, never the dependency line - NONE of the
#   twelve arrives at the line the gold pins, and only one (`Docker.php:74`, `submit`) even
#   lands in the routine that contains it. Every line below was opened and read in this
#   session; no credit is taken from a script tally.
#   THE THREE-WAY SPLIT STEP 1 NOW ASKS FOR IS THE PAYLOAD'S OWN STRUCTURE: `direct_callers`
#   46 (ask a machine to do something), `affected_via_composition` 89 (carry one as part of
#   what they are), `importers_not_reached_count` 81 (name the type, no call edge reaches
#   them) - and `completeness: {"verdict": "partial", "resolved": 135, "hidden": 163}` is the
#   "how sure are you that this is the real total" half. One call answers all four.
#   Recorded, not acted on (NO GREP SCREEN IS A GATE): all 101 payload files contain the
#   literal token `Server`, checked in this session. TOKEN-DARKNESS IS NOT THE MECHANISM - the
#   banked +0.53 cell has 16 of 16 gold files carrying its anchor token. What is being priced
#   here is the read, not the grep hit.
#
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS
#   Per scored row the answer is WHICH PIECE OF A MACHINE'S IDENTITY OUTLIVED THE MACHINE
#   BEING IN HAND, AND WHAT IT IS LATER TRUSTED TO RESOLVE TO. A search prints lines; it
#   cannot print the lifetime of a value. Two rows whose text is nearly identical differ
#   entirely on that axis: `app/Livewire/Server/Sentinel.php:86` compares a handle that
#   arrived in a broadcast payload against the one it holds, and both die with the request,
#   while `app/Models/SharedEnvironmentVariable.php:22` files an integer into a row that
#   outlives every process - and the value stored beside it was COPIED from a machine's
#   display name at creation (`app/Models/Server.php:199-:200`) and is never refreshed.
#
# THE AXIS, IN ONE SENTENCE
#   Every scored dependent kept a piece of a machine's identity past the moment it held the
#   machine and later trusts that piece to still resolve to the same machine, so its
#   dependency on the contract is carried by a value with a lifetime rather than by a call, a
#   held reference or a hand-off.
#
# MEMORIZATION (bench/lib/memorization_probe.py, quoted, run in this session)
#   Server, THIS session: {"ok": true, "ran": true, "recall": 0.0, "hits_n": 0, "truth_n": 107,
#            "said_n": 1, "samples": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0], "answer_head": "UNKNOWN\n",
#            "reason": "closed-book recall 0.0 < 0.3 - not recited"}
#   Read the payload, not the flag: `said_n: 1` and an answer of "UNKNOWN" means the probe
#   named nothing at all, so this run is a degenerate pass and not evidence of anything. The
#   informative run is attempt 7's, quoted from that header:
#     {"ok": false, "recall": 0.523, "hits_n": 56, "truth_n": 107, "said_n": 87,
#      "samples": [0.514, 0.523, 0.0], "reason": "... 0.523 (56 members named) >= 0.3 - recited"}
#   What came back recited there was the machine class's OWN member list - booted, settings,
#   destinations, applications, getContainers, forceDisableServer - every member of which lives
#   in the contract file, an ANCHOR here that does not score. That session intersected the
#   probe's `hits` against the functions defined in the twelve scored files and recorded
#   "scored files defining a recited member: 0". No row is memorized under either run, and the
#   two runs disagree by 0.523 on the same symbol, which is a note on the probe's variance
#   (`samples` carries a 0.0 in both), not on this gold.
#
# THE RISK IN THIS DRAFT, NAMED
#   The characterisation may be answerable by grep after all. `grep -rln "App\\Models\\Server\b"`
#   gives a total in one call, and an arm that reports that number three ways by eyeballing the
#   hits has satisfied step 1 cheaply - in which case the baseline arrives at step 2 as rich as
#   a6's did and lands near 0.75 again. What that would establish is that the ENUMERATION was
#   never the lever either, and the only remaining untried one is the gold group, which this
#   draft holds fixed on a run killer (above) rather than on preference. The distinguishing
#   evidence is in the transcript, not the score: whether step 1's baseline separates "holds
#   one" from "calls one" at all, and whether it reports a completeness position.
#   The measurable failure mode on OUR side is the a3/a5/a7 skip returning: three of seven
#   cycles our arm never called `sense_blast`, and in a7 it scored 0.75 entirely off greps with
#   8 of 9 rows CITED-NOT-RETURNED. A step 1 that asks for a split and a completeness position
#   is the strongest pull toward the structural call this campaign has written, but it is a
#   pull, not a requirement - the prompt may not name a tool. `tool_use_audit` on the step-1
#   window is what says which happened, and a 0.75 reached by grep is not a Sense result
#   whatever the delta says.
#   Third risk, carried over: two rows (`d:shared-env-var`, `d:ssl-certificate`) are each a
#   `'server_id',` line in a fillable array, so one `grep -rn "'server_id',"` prints both -
#   2 of 12 = 0.17 of the group to one search. Fourth: `grep -rn "serverUuid" app` prints 12
#   hits over 6 files, one of them `d:sentinel-event`. Recorded, not acted on.
#
# ONE DEFECT FIXED IN ATTEMPT 5 AND STILL IN FORCE
#   `d:cloud-token-check` matches on `Server/CloudProviderToken/Show.php`, narrowed because
#   `CloudProviderToken/Show.php` alone also matches
#   `app/Livewire/Security/CloudProviderToken/Show.php`. Every match fragment in this gold was
#   re-checked against `git ls-files` and returns exactly 1.
# ============================================================================

name: Coolify work session - audit the server contract before a rework
repo: coolify
contract_symbol: Server
contract_file: app/Models/Server.php

description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework what identifies one of the remote machines this
  product deploys onto. Today a machine is a row with a number, a short handle, a display
  name and a network address, and code all over the product treats any one of those as a
  durable way of getting back to it later. The rework breaks that: a machine will be able to
  be renamed, re-addressed and replaced by a different one standing in for it, so a piece of
  identity taken now is no longer guaranteed to mean the same machine when it is read back.
  Before you touch it you have to know two things: how a machine's identity is established,
  changed and torn down today, and how far the change reaches - in particular every place
  that kept one of those pieces past the moment it had the machine itself, because those are
  the places that will silently point at the wrong machine, or at no machine, without a single
  line of them changing. Throughout, an answer that points at a place must give an exact
  file:line a teammate could jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare
  filename does not count, a set of names offered together with one shared reason and no
  location on any of them does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.

steps:
  - name: Map the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: orient, and be thorough - the rest of the ticket is built on this and nothing
      later will send you back for it. Two things, both in this answer.
      First, what identifies one of these machines, and where that identity is established,
      changed and destroyed. Trace it end to end: where the identifying fields are normalised
      as the record is written and where the previous value of the changing one is preserved;
      where a freshly created machine causes further records to be seeded from it, including
      any that take a COPY of an identifying field rather than a link back to it; where a
      lookup by identity is served out of a per-request cache instead of the database, and
      where that cache is emptied; and what the removal path does and does not clean up behind
      it.
      Second, and this is what tells me how big the rework is: the SHAPE of everything around
      it. Not the list - I am not asking you to write the places out. I am asking what the
      population looks like. How many distinct places in this product reach one of these
      machines at all, and how does that total split three ways: the places that ask a machine
      to do something; the places that carry a machine around as part of what they themselves
      are, whether or not they ever ask it anything; and the places that name this kind of
      thing and never ask it for anything at all. Give me the three figures and the total, say
      which of the three is the largest and roughly by how much, and say how sure you are that
      the total is the real total rather than as far as you got - and on what basis.
      Then, and only for these, the hard cases: the places you could not confidently assign to
      one of the three, at exact file:line, each with one phrase on what made the call
      ambiguous. Those are the ones I will have to rule on myself.
      Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework of identity would have to preserve for the
      population you have just described to keep meaning what it means today. A figure offered
      without saying how you arrived at it does not count, and neither does a hard case named
      without the file:line where it is defined.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "10"
        required: true
        description: The identity lifecycle traced through the routines that establish, change and tear it down, and the surrounding population characterised by size, by how each place reaches a machine, and by how complete the figure is
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_graph
        required: false
        layer: adoption

  - name: Audit every dependent of the server contract
    prompt: >
      Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the first
      part already cost you. You know the shape of the population that reaches a machine
      directly. Now go past its edge, and find every place that KEPT something of a machine's
      identity after the machine
      itself was gone from its hands, and later trusts that keepsake to still mean the same
      machine when it comes back to it - a request later, a restart later, or in another
      system entirely. That trust is exactly what the rework withdraws, and nothing in such a
      place changes shape when it breaks. A place like that is often doing some other job
      altogether and may never be handed a machine at all; nothing on the line says a remote
      machine is involved, only that a value was kept and outlived the thing it was taken
      from. So for every one of them the answer has three parts: the exact file:line where
      that value is taken, written or read back, and the routine that line sits in; how long
      the value lives - inside one request, inside a stored record, or outside this product
      entirely; and one phrase on what this place starts getting wrong the first time the
      value no longer resolves to the machine it came from. If you run short, say what you had
      still not looked at rather than closing on what you have. A file:line with no routine
      named around it does not count, a place named without saying what it kept does not
      count, and a missed one is a regression shipped.
    checks:
      - type: response_richness
        value: "6"
        required: true
        description: Every identity-keeping place located at file:line with what it kept, its lifetime and its own consequence
      - type: no_grep
        value: no_grep
        required: false
        layer: adoption
      - type: mcp_tool_used
        value: sense_blast
        required: false
        layer: adoption

scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.7
    efficiency: 0.3

gold:
  # ---- ANCHORS: where identity is established, cached and torn down. Step 1's answer, and
  # ---- the map step 2 tells the agent to build first. Both arms reached all four in
  # ---- attempt 3 (contract 4/4 on both) and they do NOT score. Every line below was opened
  # ---- and read in this session. None of these four files appears in `dependents`.
  - id: contract-model
    group: contract
    match: [Models/Server.php]
    relation: "app/Models/Server.php:135 `protected static function booted()` - the identity lifecycle in one place: the saving hook preserves the old address at :147 `$payload['ip_previous'] = $server->getOriginal('ip');` when it changes, and the created hook at :157 seeds the settings row at :158, a destination at :170/:174, and then TWO stored variables that take a COPY of identity rather than a link - :191 `'key' => 'COOLIFY_SERVER_UUID'` with `'value' => $server->uuid,` at :192, and :199 `'key' => 'COOLIFY_SERVER_NAME'` with `'value' => $server->name,` at :200, both written once and never refreshed"
  - id: c:identity-map
    group: contract
    match: [Models/StandaloneDocker.php]
    relation: "app/Models/StandaloneDocker.php:135 `$server = Server::findCached($this->server_id);` inside `getServerAttribute(): ?Server` (def at :127, eager-loaded short-circuit at :130-:132) - the read side of identity: a stored integer is turned back into a machine through the per-request identity map declared at `app/Models/Server.php:131` `private static ?array $identityMapCache = null;` and served by `findCached()` at :230, filled at :241 and emptied only by `flushIdentityMap()` at :251; this is the mechanism every stored id in the product bottoms out in"
  - id: c:server-setting
    group: contract
    match: [Models/ServerSetting.php]
    relation: "app/Models/ServerSetting.php:59 `'server_id',` in the fillable list, with `public function server()` at :247 returning the belongsTo - the settings record created for every machine at `app/Models/Server.php:158`, the simplest example of identity kept as a link rather than as a copy"
  - id: c:server-delete
    group: contract
    match: [Server/Delete.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/Delete.php:60 `$this->server->delete();` inside `delete(...)`, followed at :61 by `DeleteServer::dispatch(` handed nine SCALARS across :62-:70 - the id at :62, the cloud-provider instance ids at :64/:68/:70, the token id at :65 and the team id at :66 - rather than the machine; the teardown path, and the point after which every piece of identity kept elsewhere refers to a machine that no longer exists"

  # ---- DEPENDENTS: twelve rows, ONE PER FILE, across eleven areas
  # ---- (app/Notifications/Container, app/Models, app/Livewire/Project/New,
  # ---- app/Livewire/Destination/New, app/Livewire, app/Livewire/Project/Shared,
  # ---- app/Livewire/Server/CaCertificate, app/Jobs,
  # ---- app/Livewire/Server/CloudProviderToken, app/Notifications/Server,
  # ---- app/Livewire/Server).
  # ---- Every row is in the shown `sense_blast` payload for `Server` quoted in the header,
  # ---- and every line below was opened and read in this session.
  # ---- Ten of the twelve carry a measured sense citation; none carries a baseline citation
  # ---- under an ask that excludes the near edge. Provenance per row is in the header.

  # -- a piece of identity copied into a message that leaves this product for a system we do
  # -- not control. The copy is taken at render time and is never revisited.
  - id: d:container-stopped
    group: dependents
    match: [Container/ContainerStopped.php]
    relation: "app/Notifications/Container/ContainerStopped.php:113 `'server_name' => $this->server->name,` with `'server_uuid' => $this->server->uuid,` at :114, inside the `$data` array opened at :108 in `toWebhook(): array` (def at :106) - an alert renders a machine's display name and handle into a payload posted to somebody else's endpoint; those keys are a contract other systems parse, so a rename makes every already-delivered alert refer to a machine nobody can find, and a stand-in machine makes them refer to the wrong one"
  - id: d:patch-check-link
    group: dependents
    match: [Server/ServerPatchCheck.php]
    relation: "app/Notifications/Server/ServerPatchCheck.php:19 `$this->serverUrl = base_url().'/server/'.$this->server->uuid.'/security/patches';` in `__construct(public Server $server, public array $patchData)` (def at :16, queued onto 'high' at :18) - the handle is baked into a deep link at construction time and stored in the `public string $serverUrl` declared at :14; the notification is queued, so the link is composed before delivery and then sits in somebody's mailbox indefinitely, pointing at whatever that handle resolves to when it is finally clicked"

  # -- a bare number filed into a stored row. The row outlives every process, and the line
  # -- that keeps it says nothing about a remote machine at all.
  - id: d:shared-env-var
    group: dependents
    match: [Models/SharedEnvironmentVariable.php]
    relation: "app/Models/SharedEnvironmentVariable.php:22 `'server_id',` in the fillable list, under the `// Type and relationships` comment at :17 and beside `'team_id',` :19 / `'project_id',` :20 / `'environment_id',` :21 - two rows of this table are created at `app/Models/Server.php:190` and `:198` whose stored VALUE is a copy of the machine's handle and display name, so this table holds identity twice over: once as a link a rename follows, and once as a value frozen at creation that it does not"
  - id: d:ssl-certificate
    group: dependents
    match: [Models/SslCertificate.php]
    relation: "app/Models/SslCertificate.php:16 `'server_id',` in the fillable list opened at :9, sitting between `'resource_id',` at :15 and `'common_name',` at :17 - a generated certificate is filed against the machine it was issued for, while the certificate material at :10-:11 is only valid where it was installed; the row survives the machine, so a re-used number hands one machine's private key to another"

  # -- a piece of identity carried through a redirect or a form step as a loose parameter,
  # -- then written into whatever gets created at the end of the flow.
  - id: d:new-resource-select
    group: dependents
    match: [New/Select.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/New/Select.php:441 `'server_id' => $this->server_id,` inside the `redirect()->route('project.resource.create', [` built at :436 in `setPostgresqlType(string $type)` (def at :432), and repeated at :456 in `whatToDoNext()` (def at :446) - the chosen machine survives the wizard as a bare number in a URL across a full browser round trip; nothing in this component holds a machine between steps, only the number does"
  - id: d:new-destination
    group: dependents
    match: [Destination/New/Docker.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Destination/New/Docker.php:87 `'server_id' => $this->selectedServer->id,` inside `SwarmDocker::create([` at :84 on the swarm branch of `submit(): mixed` (def at :74), and again at :98 inside `StandaloneDocker::create([` at :95 - the moment a machine's identity becomes a permanent row: the destination created here is what every deployed resource later walks through to find its machine, and it stores the number, not the machine"
  - id: d:additional-network-pivot
    group: dependents
    match: [Shared/Destination.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Project/Shared/Destination.php:130 `$this->resource->additional_networks()->attach($mainDestination->id, ['server_id' => $mainDestination->server->id]);` inside the transaction opened at :121 in `promote(int $network_id, int $server_id)` (def at :115), with the matching detach keyed on the same number at :128 `->wherePivot('server_id', $server->id)` - the number is stamped into the PIVOT row itself, so a resource's extra machines are recorded nowhere but in a join table; the id also arrives as a bare int parameter at :115 and is only resolved back at :118"

  # -- a piece of identity copied outward into a projection this product itself reads back.
  - id: d:global-search
    group: dependents
    match: [Livewire/GlobalSearch.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/GlobalSearch.php:521 `'id' => $server->id,` opening the entry built by the `->map(function ($server) {` closure at :519 over `Server::ownedByCurrentTeam()->get()` at :517-:518, spanning :520-:530 - the projection flattens a machine into a search record carrying its display name at :522, its handle at :524, a link at :526 and a `search_text` blob at :529 concatenating the name, the ADDRESS and the description; every field is a copy that goes stale the moment the machine is renamed or re-addressed"

  # -- a piece of identity handed to something that runs later, or belonging to a system this
  # -- product does not own and cannot re-resolve.
  - id: d:ca-certificate
    group: dependents
    match: [CaCertificate/Show.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/CaCertificate/Show.php:82 `server_id: $this->server->id,` as a named argument to `dispatch(new RegenerateSslCertJob(` opened at :81, after `writeCertificateToServer()` at :79 - the machine is reduced to a number at the moment work is queued, so the certificate is regenerated against whatever that number resolves to when the queue gets to it, not against the machine the operator was looking at"
  - id: d:cloud-status-check
    group: dependents
    match: [ServerCloudProviderStatusCheckJob.php]
    relation: "app/Jobs/ServerCloudProviderStatusCheckJob.php:41 `'hetzner' => $this->server->hetzner_server_id` inside the `match (...)` at :40 in `handle(): void` (def at :33), with the siblings at :44 `'vultr' => $this->server->vultr_instance_id` and :47 `'digitalocean' => $this->server->digitalocean_droplet_id` - identity belonging to a system we do not own, stored on our row so the same machine can be named over there; the job also composes the handle into an overlap key at :30 `new WithoutOverlapping('server-cloud-provider-status-'.$this->server->uuid)` that outlives the run by 130 seconds, and if a machine is replaced by a stand-in these foreign ids still point at the old provider instance"
  - id: d:cloud-token-check
    group: dependents
    match: [Server/CloudProviderToken/Show.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/CloudProviderToken/Show.php:117 `])->timeout(10)->get(\"https://api.hetzner.cloud/v1/servers/{$this->server->hetzner_server_id}\");` in the `Http::withHeaders([` call opened at :115, guarded by `if ($this->server->hetzner_server_id)` at :114 - the foreign identifier is pasted straight into a URL against somebody else's API, and the answer decides whether the operator is told the token 'cannot access this server' at :121-:122; the product cannot re-resolve that identifier from its own side"

  # -- a piece of identity that arrives from outside the process as a loose string and is
  # -- trusted to be comparable to the one held here.
  - id: d:sentinel-event
    group: dependents
    match: [Server/Sentinel.php]
    relation: "app/Livewire/Server/Sentinel.php:86 `if ($event['serverUuid'] === $this->server->uuid) {` inside `handleSentinelRestarted($event)` (def at :84) - the handle travels out of this process and comes back in a broadcast payload, and the identity test is a string comparison against the machine this component holds; whether the refresh at :87 and the success message at :91 fire for the operator watching depends entirely on that string still naming the same machine at both ends"
